WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
Where on a 300-year-old oak were this spring's new leaves made?

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.220
The tree is ancient, but this spring's leaves are only months old.

3
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:14.943
The answer is meristems.

4
00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:19.083
A meristem is a group of cells that keeps dividing.

5
00:00:20.443 --> 00:00:25.163
No adult mammal grows a new limb, but many plants keep their meristems for years.

6
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:27.903
So they keep adding new organs.

7
00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:32.797
Apical meristems sit at the tips of growing roots and shoots.

8
00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:36.297
Growth from them is called primary growth.

9
00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:38.917
It is mainly growth in length.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.600
Zoom in on a root tip.

11
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.640
A root cap shields the front as it pushes through soil.

12
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.280
Just behind the cap, small cells divide.

13
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.060
This is the zone of division.

14
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:55.943
A little further back, division tapers off.

15
00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:02.123
Cells lengthen many times over as water fills their vacuoles, the big water sacs

16
00:01:02.123 --> 00:01:05.823
inside them. This is the zone of elongation.

17
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:09.463
Further back still, cells stop lengthening.

18
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:14.423
They take on their final types, and root hairs grow out from the surface.

19
00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:17.323
This is the zone of maturation.

20
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:21.723
Now follow one lengthwise row of cells, a cell file.

21
00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:25.743
New cells leave the meristem at a steady rhythm.

22
00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:28.683
Each one stretches, then stops.

23
00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:32.503
Meanwhile the tip keeps moving forward through the soil.

24
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:37.067
Paint ink marks at even spacing along a growing root tip.

25
00:01:38.327 --> 00:01:40.907
A day later, which gaps between the marks have widened?

26
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.990
Mostly the gaps in the zone of elongation, where cells stretch.

27
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.350
In the mature region, where cells have stopped lengthening, the marks stay put.

28
00:01:57.433 --> 00:01:59.313
So how fast does the root grow?

29
00:02:00.713 --> 00:02:02.913
Count the new cells each file makes per hour.

30
00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.353
Multiply by the final length each cell reaches.

31
00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:09.507
That product is the growth rate.

32
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:15.287
It holds at steady state, when the zones keep their size and every file behaves

33
00:02:15.287 --> 00:02:16.287
alike.

34
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:20.217
Now block stretching but leave division alone.

35
00:02:21.817 --> 00:02:24.357
Cells keep coming, yet the tip barely creeps forward.

36
00:02:25.677 --> 00:02:29.297
Most of a root's new length comes from stretching, not from dividing.

37
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.630
A shoot tip grows the same way, though it has no cap.

38
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:39.210
At each node it adds one or more leaves, with a bud in the angle of each leaf.

39
00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:43.093
So a plant's new cells come from its meristems.

40
00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:49.333
For a root at steady state, growth equals new cells per file per hour, times their

41
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:50.073
final length.
